      "id": "concept-01-test-isolation",
      "category": "best-practices",
      "prompt": "What are the best practices for test data isolation in Playwright?",
      "answer": "The best practices for test data isolation in Playwright include:\n\n1. **Dynamic API-driven seeding**: Every test suite creates its own workspaces, users, and contexts via REST/GraphQL, appending a cryptographic seed or timestamp (e.g., `test-uuid-${Date.now()}`) to every name so records cannot collide.\n\n2. **Isolated browser context pooling**: Each Playwright worker runs in its own browser context. Cookies, localStorage, and IndexedDB are scoped to the context, preventing bleed between parallel tests.\n\n3. **Fixture and cleanup contracts**: Any fixture that creates state must register a teardown, preferably via Playwright’s `auto` fixture pattern so cleanup runs even on test failure. Use API DELETE rather than UI flows for teardown speed.\n\nThese practices isolate data per test, preventing race conditions and flakiness that arise from shared state.",
