      "id": "concept-02-prompt-injection-types",
      "category": "best-practices",
      "prompt": "What are the main categories of prompt injection attacks that QA should test for?",
      "answer": "Based on the provided context, the main categories of prompt injection attacks that QA should test for are:\n\n1. **Direct** - User text overrides system prompt.\n2. **Indirect (retrieved)** - Malicious instructions hidden in PDFs, scraped pages, emails the agent reads.\n3. **Indirect (uploaded file)** - Same payload, hidden in a user-uploaded `.txt` / `.pdf` / `.docx` / image alt-text the chatbot parses.\n4. **Data extraction** - Roleplay/reverse psychology coaxes training data or secrets.\n5. **Tool-call hijack** - Crafted input causes an agentic system to call sensitive tools with attacker args.\n6. **Crescendo / multi-turn** - Each turn alone is benign; escalation across N turns lands the unsafe ask.\n7. **Dual-use safety framing** - Harmful ask reframed as protective.\n8. **Homoglyph / encoded** - Cyrillic look-alikes, base64, ROT13, zero-width chars bypass substring filters.\n\nThese categories cover various ways attackers can manipulate the model's behavior through prompt injection.",
